1. AWS Account Setup
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Install and configure AWS CLI:

curl "https://awscli.amazonaws.com/awscli-exe-linux-x86_64.zip" -o "awscliv2.zip"
unzip awscliv2.zip
sudo ./aws/install
aws configure

provide the AWS user secrete, key, region and zone details.

Your IAM user needs permissions for:

EC2, IAM, Route53, S3, and ELB. Domain Setup

========================================================================================

2. Register or use an existing domain (e.g., manipalmhea.com).
Verify DNS with:

nslookup manipalmheaopp.com
dig .manipalmheaopp.com NS


=========================================================================================
You can manage DNS directly in Route53 for simplicity.


3. AWS Quota Check
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
OpenShift requires multiple vCPUs by default. Check your quotas and increase base on your requirement if reqired:

Example:

aws service-quotas get-service-quota \
  --service-code ec2 \
  --quota-code L-1216C47A \
  --region us-west-2

================================================================================================
4. OpenShift Installation
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
a) Download Tools: #w.r.t the OCP version 

Install Openshift installer
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
wget https://mirror.openshift.com/pub/openshift-v4/clients/ocp/latest/openshift-install-linux.tar.gz
tar -xzf openshift-install-linux.tar.gz
chmod +x openshift-install
sudo mv openshift-install /usr/local/bin/
./openshift-install version

Install oc CLI tools:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
wget https://mirror.openshift.com/pub/openshift-v4/clients/ocp/latest/openshift-client-linux.tar.gz
tar -xzf openshift-client-linux.tar.gz
sudo mv oc kubectl /usr/local/bin/
oc version


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
b) Generate SSH Key
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -f ./openshift-key -N ""
cat ./openshift-key.pub

Keep your private key safe — you’ll need it for node access.

c) Get the Pull Secret
Get it from Red Hat OpenShift Cluster Manager.
You’ll paste this JSON secret during installation in the install-config file


d) create Clusterconfig directory
mkdir clusterconfig

e) Create Install Config

openshift-install create install-config --dir=.

Respond to the interactive prompts:

Platform: aws

Region: ap-south-1

Base Domain: manipalmheaopp.com

Cluster Name: mheaoppaws

SSH key & Pull secret: paste from earlier steps






f) Edit install-config.yaml:

apiVersion: v1
baseDomain: manipalmheaopp.com
compute:
- architecture: amd64
  hyperthreading: Enabled
  name: worker
  platform:
    aws:
      type: m7i.4xlarge
  replicas: 3
controlPlane:
  architecture: amd64
  hyperthreading: Enabled
  name: master
  platform:
     aws:
       type: m7i.xlarge
  replicas: 3
metadata:
  name: mheaoppaws
networking:
  clusterNetwork:
  - cidr: 10.128.0.0/14
    hostPrefix: 23
  machineNetwork:
  - cidr: 10.0.0.0/16
  networkType: OVNKubernetes
  serviceNetwork:
  - 172.30.0.0/16
platform:
  aws:
    region: ap-south-1
publish: External
pullSecret: ''
sshKey: ''


cp install-config.yaml install-config.yaml.backup
./openshift-install create cluster --dir=. --log-level=info

This will Create VPC, subnets, security groups, and load balancers

Launch control plane and worker nodes

Configure DNS and bootstrap cluster services

You can monitor logs:
tail -f .openshift_install.log


Access the Cluster
export KUBECONFIG=./auth/kubeconfig
oc get nodes

Web Console Access
cat ./auth/kubeadmin-password
oc get route console -n openshift-console

Login at the provided URL using:
Username: kubeadmin
Password: (from above)
